# Refactoring Guidelines

## When to Refactor

- Large functions (> 50 lines)
- Large files (> 400 lines)
- Code duplication
- Complex or hard-to-read logic
- Poor naming
- Performance bottlenecks (CPU, memory, I/O)
- Tight coupling between modules / layers
- Inconsistent error handling or state management

## Refactoring Workflow

1. Ensure tests cover current behaviour
2. Plan small, incremental steps
3. Keep behaviour stable – run tests after every change
4. Update type hints, docs and comments alongside code changes
5. Submit changes for code review
6. Verify performance and monitor in production

## Common Patterns

- Extract function / method
- Simplify conditionals
- Consolidate duplicate code (DRY)
- Introduce data classes or DTOs for related fields
- Encapsulate external calls behind clean interfaces
- Standardise error handling with helper utilities

### Example

```python
# Before

def process_order(data):
    # long function handling validation, transformation,
    # persistence and notification
    ...

# After

def process_order(data):
    validated = _validate(data)
    transformed = _transform(validated)
    _persist(transformed)
    _notify()
```

## Tooling

- Static type checking: mypy
- Linting & formatting: ruff / flake8 / black
- Complexity analysis: radon (or similar)
- Dependency analysis: pipdeptree
- Test coverage: pytest + coverage

## Checklist

- [ ] Tests in place and passing
- [ ] Behaviour unchanged
- [ ] Readability improved
- [ ] No code duplication
- [ ] Docs & type hints updated
- [ ] Performance acceptable
